r and Miller (1983) showed that strongly reducing gas inventories favor amino-acid diversity, whereas CO2 without H2 is ineffective for comparable complex products. This is direct reachability from simple feedstocks, but not a selective reaction or a quantified continuous source of free glutamate. The lumped chemistry may overlap mechanistically with the explicit Strecker branch; these are distinct network representations, not proof of independent microscopic mechanisms."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Impact-driven glutamate formation from bicarbonate and ammonium", "inputs": ["mol_033", "mol_002", "mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fe/Ni-bearing meteoritic material; reactive iron is a reducing inventory rather than an established trace catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Extreme transient impact heating followed by quenching into approximately 300-350 K water; the supplied summary does not specify the shock temperature profile", "pressure": "Transient high shock pressure followed by return toward ambient pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Surface denotes the impact-recovery setting, not a claim that shock conditions remain within ordinary pond limits. Bicarbonate, ammonium-bearing water, and meteoritic material are present. N2 may be present as atmosphere, but its incorporation is not assigned when ammonium is available."}, "mechanism": "Lumped shock, redox, carbon-nitrogen assembly, and quench chemistry produces a complex recovered organic mixture containing glutamate. Fe(0)-to-Fe(II) is a formal reducing-equivalent endpoint; actual oxidized iron phases and intermediate organic structures are unresolved.", "reasoning": "Furukawa et al. (2015) detected glutamate among impact-recovery products from bicarbonate-containing aqueous nitrogen sources and meteoritic materials. This supports access from inorganic carbon without externally supplied alpha-ketoglutarate. The supplied research gives no glutamate-specific yield, elementary mechanism, or complete iron-speciation balance. The graph therefore represents an empirical synthesis module, not a proven single stoichiometric reaction. Episodicity, rapid quenching, product work-up, and poor selectivity limit its interpretation as a sustained source."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Iron-built carbon skeleton followed by H2/Ni reductive amination", "description": "Pyruvate and glyoxylate generate an alpha-ketoglutarate-containing pool, which is cooled and exposed to ammonia and pressurized H2 over nickel. The modules are independently supported, but their complete serial integration and concentration balance are unproven.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Iron-built carbon skeleton followed by electroreduced-mineral amination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the carbon-building network is aminated using preconditioned, highly reduced iron-sulfide material. The distinct reducing inventory is consumed, and its natural preparation is an explicit unresolved prerequisite.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Iron-built carbon skeleton followed by ZnS photochemical amination", "description": "An alpha-ketoglutarate-containing pool is cooled, adjusted to alkaline ammonium-rich conditions, and illuminated on ZnS with sulfite. This is a staged proposal requiring mineral and solution conditioning, not a demonstrated single-pot synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Integrated ketoacid-to-oxime-to-glutamate sequence", "description": "Iron-promoted carbon chemistry generates alpha-ketoglutarate, hydroxylamine converts it to an oxime, and Fe(0) supplies the four-electron reduction to glutamate. This has relatively strong experimental network integration but an unresolved hydroxylamine supply.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Strecker-derived glycine feeding aluminum-assisted transamination", "description": "Parallel precursor branches supply glycine from formaldehyde/ammonia/cyanide and alpha-ketoglutarate from pyruvate/glyoxylate. Their acidic Al(III)-assisted convergence gives glutamate and regenerates glyoxylate. The sequence is dependency-ordered assembly of both branches, not a claim that every successive reaction shares a single linear intermediate. An initial glyoxylate inventory is still required.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_001", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019", "mol_020", "mol_005", "mol_004"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "DAP-supported Bucherer-Bergs synthesis and hydantoin hydrolysis", "description": "A DAP-derived ammonia source and an alpha-ketoglutarate-producing branch feed concentrated cyanide/bicarbonate chemistry. Hydantoin formation efficiently traps the skeleton, but ring opening and decarbamoylation slowly release free glutamate. The sequence includes the parallel ammonia-source prerequisite.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_001", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_023", "mol_024"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Internal-redox N-succinyl pathway with conditional hydrolytic release", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate and ammonium generate N-succinylglutamate through decarboxylating internal-redox chemistry. Subsequent amide hydrolysis produces free glutamate and succinate. The precursor is supported experimentally, whereas efficient natural release of free glutamate remains speculative.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_025"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Alpha-ketoglutarate-independent acrolein/dinitrile pathway", "description"