fidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"The carbon-skeleton construction and aminodinitrile formula are coherent, and every listed step is balanced or balanceable. Van Trump and Miller (1972) support the acrolein-based proposal, while Fern\u00e1ndez-Garc\u00eda et al. (2017) provide strong experimental support for the cyanide-addition/cyanohydrin module. Their approximately 95% intermediate yield is not a glutamate yield. Conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir to the aminodinitrile is plausible but less directly constrained by the supplied evidence, and exhaustive hydrolysis of both nitriles requires specified conditions. Partial hydrolysis can retain amide products rather than produce free glutamate.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Document the initial concentration regime, including the demonstrated approximately 140 mM acrolein, 700 mM cyanide and pH 9.2 conditions, or substantiate a less concentrated alternative.\",\n        \"Confirm the aminodinitrile structure and formation yield under the proposed ammonia/cyanide conditions instead of assuming quantitative conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir.\",\n        \"Specify and test exhaustive hydrolysis, tracking both nitrile-derived groups, intermediate amides and free glutamate. Include the checked four-water and two-ammonia coefficients in rxn_019.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.0\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.0,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.28,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.51,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.22,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.25,\n      \"feedback\": \"rxn_020 is UNBALANCED according to the supplied deterministic check and must therefore be treated as a conservation violation. Calling it a lumped transformation does not exempt it from that requirement. This is a defect in the submitted reaction representation, not evidence against discharge synthesis: Ring et al. (1972) directly identified glutamic acid in discharge products. The check suggests NH3 as a possible coproduct and CO2 or CO as possible reactants; these are diagnostic candidates, not an experimentally justified correction. Its H2-product hint is insufficient by itself because H2 is already listed. Gas composition, discharge energy and aqueous recovery conditions also remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Replace rxn_020 with an experimentally defensible, coefficient-bearing net reaction or expanded reaction module containing the necessary coproducts, and rerun the required conservation check.\",\n        \"Investigate the checker-indicated NH3, CO2 and CO possibilities against the experimental material balance rather than adding species solely to obtain a formal pass.\",\n        \"Specify the reducing gas mixture, energy deposition and recovery procedure, including whether hydrolysis is required before free glutamate is measured.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.72\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.72,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.7,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.54,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.76,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.68,\n      \"feedback\": \"The supplied deterministic check reports rxn_021 as balanceable, which is accepted. Furukawa et al. (2015) provide experimental evidence that bicarbonate/nitrogen-containing impact mixtures with meteoritic minerals can yield glutamate. The acknowledged lumped representation is reasonable for qualitative reachability, but the inferred coefficients do not establish an experimental product distribution or a resolved mechanism. Formal Fe/Fe2+ and proton bookkeeping should not be interpreted as freely dissolved iron or a strongly acidic impact solution. The network lacks the shock conditions, mineral composition, quench history and recovery details necessary to evaluate this particular environmental implementation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify shock pressure, temperature-time history, mineral phases and loadings, initial solution composition and quench conditions corresponding to the cited experiments.\",\n        \"Distinguish measured free glutamate from any glutamate recovered only after analytical hydrolysis, and report a glutamate-specific yield or detection limit.\",\n        \"Retain explicit labeling as an empirical mixed-product module; document its material balance and post-impact collection without assuming that formal coefficients describe actual mineral consumption.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is structurally substantial and connected, uses molecule IDs consistently, and contains several experimentally supported routes to stereochemically unspecified glutamic acid. The required balance assessment gives 11 reactions balanced as written, nine balanceable with coefficients, and one conservation violation: rxn_020. Correcting rxn_020 is mandatory; its failure does not invalidate the independently supported branches. Explicit coefficients should be added to the nine balanceable reactions. Literature alignment is strong at the reaction-family level, especially for the integrated iron/oxime module, direct reductive aminations, transamination and Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. However, the submitted reaction entries contain no attached citations or quantitative operating conditions. Surface and Biochemical labels alone cannot establish environmental consistency, catalyst availability or transfer feasibility. Principal network-level uncertainties are usable 2-oxoglutarate production in the upstream mixture, retention and conditioning between modules, concentration requirements, active mineral inventories, and release of free glutamate from protected intermediates. Conditional feedstocks are appropriately acknowledged rather than falsely presented as geochemically closed supplies; glyoxylate should nevertheless be explicitly marked as conditionally externally supplied despite its intermediate label. Formal protonation and iron bookkeeping are accepted, and neither stereochemical ambiguity nor the number of pathways, shared molecules or environments is penalized. P4 ranks highest because upstream-to-target chemical compatibility has experimental support. Problematic-reaction listings otherwise identify unresolved specification or integration issues, not additional conservation failures. The network is a credible conditional synthesis map, but it is not approved under the required overall-confidence threshold of 0.90.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.84, "rxn_002": 0.9}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.87, "pathway_coherence": 0.87, "environmental_progression": 0.64, "target_achievement": 0.89, "overall_confidence": 0.82, "feedback": "Both modules have experimental support: Muchowska et al. (2019) demonstrate an iron-promoted network containing 2-oxoglutarate, and Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrate its H2-driven conversion to glutamate over suitable metals. rxn_001 is balanceable, but its inferred coefficients are not established experimental product ratios. The main integration gap is delivery of sufficiently concentrated 2-oxoglutarate from the upstream mixture to an active Ni/H2 system. The Biochemical label does not specify a physical environment, and the submitted network provides no reaction-specific pressure, pH, catalyst loading or residence time. Competing hydroxyglutarate formation should be included when assessing productivity.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002"], "suggestions": ["Parameterize rxn_001 with its Fe(II) inventory, approximately 70\u00b0C experimental conditions, residence time and measured 2-oxoglutarate production.", "For rxn_002, document conditions comparable to the demonstrated pH 8, 22\u00b0C, 5 bar H2 and 72-hour experiment, or justify departures. Specify the active Ni-bearing material.", "Demonstrate conditioning or transfer of the upstream mixture with adequate substrate retention, and measure amino-acid versus hydroxyacid selectivity."]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.84, "rxn_003": 0.85}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.85, "pathway_coherence": 0.84, "environmental_progression": 0.62, "target_achievement": 0.89, "overall_confidence": 0.8, "feedback": "Kitadai et al. (2019) provide strong evidence for glutamate formation using partially electroreduced FeS. The network appropriately acknowledges preconditioned mineral availability as conditional. The Fe/Fe2+ representation is acceptable formal electron bookkeeping, but it does not identify the actual reactive surface or establish that ordinary FeS or arbitrary bulk iron would reproduce the result. The literature used deliberately prepared material, alkaline conditions and substantial ammonium excess. Compatibility with the upstream iron-network mixture and the physical meaning of the Biochemical environment remain unspecified.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "suggestions": ["Specify the reduced FeS phase, loading and preparation history, including the experimental benchmark of approximately -0.7 V versus SHE for seven days.", "Provide the amination pH, temperature, ammonium concentration and residence time; distinguish formal Fe2+ bookkeeping from actual dissolved and solid iron products.", "Test the conditioned upstream mixture or justify transfer, dilution and mineral contact sufficiently to support the proposed coupling."]}, {"pathway