during the thermal progression.", "Measure DAP consumption, ammonia release, hydantoin, N-carbamoylglutamate and free glutamate separately; do not equate hydantoin yield with target yield."]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.84, "rxn_014": 0.81, "rxn_015": 0.55}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.66, "pathway_coherence": 0.78, "environmental_progression": 0.44, "target_achievement": 0.52, "overall_confidence": 0.6, "feedback": "Yanagawa and co-workers provide experimental precedent for N-succinylglutamate formation. rxn_014 is balanceable using two equivalents of 2-oxoglutarate, and its internal-redox strategy does not require inventing an external reductant. The principal weakness is rxn_015: formation of an N-acyl derivative does not establish environmentally effective release of free glutamate. Strong-acid hydrolysis was important in analogous literature experiments, while glutamate-specific liberation under mild conditions remains poorly constrained. The network acknowledges this conditional release, but does not provide conditions or kinetics that resolve it. Competing pyroglutamate formation also limits straightforward target accounting.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_015"], "suggestions": ["Write the two-2-oxoglutarate stoichiometry of rxn_014 explicitly and specify the acidic formation conditions.", "Demonstrate rxn_015 under a defined environmental pH, temperature and residence time; distinguish a laboratory strong-acid work-up from spontaneous environmental release.", "Quantify free glutamate, N-succinylglutamate and pyroglutamate separately. Treat this branch as a derivative reservoir unless adequate free-target release is demonstrated."]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_016": 0.89, "rxn_017": 0.92, "rxn_018": 0.79, "rxn_019": 0.71}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.82, "pathway_coherence": 0.87, "environmental_progression": 0.6, "target_achievement": 0.75, "overall_confidence": 0.76, "feedback": "The carbon-skeleton construction and aminodinitrile formula are coherent, and every listed step is balanced or balanceable. Van Trump and Miller (1972) support the acrolein-based proposal, while Fern\u00e1ndez-Garc\u00eda et al. (2017) provide strong experimental support for the cyanide-addition/cyanohydrin module. Their approximately 95% intermediate yield is not a glutamate yield. Conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir to the aminodinitrile is plausible but less directly constrained by the supplied evidence, and exhaustive hydrolysis of both nitriles requires specified conditions. Partial hydrolysis can retain amide products rather than produce free glutamate.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "suggestions": ["Document the initial concentration regime, including the demonstrated approximately 140 mM acrolein, 700 mM cyanide and pH 9.2 conditions, or substantiate a less concentrated alternative.", "Confirm the aminodinitrile structure and formation yield under the proposed ammonia/cyanide conditions instead of assuming quantitative conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir.", "Specify and test exhaustive hydrolysis, tracking both nitrile-derived groups, intermediate amides and free glutamate. Include the checked four-water and two-ammonia coefficients in rxn_019."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_020": 0.0}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.0, "pathway_coherence": 0.28, "environmental_progression": 0.51, "target_achievement": 0.22, "overall_confidence": 0.25, "feedback": "rxn_020 is UNBALANCED according to the supplied deterministic check and must therefore be treated as a conservation violation. Calling it a lumped transformation does not exempt it from that requirement. This is a defect in the submitted reaction representation, not evidence against discharge synthesis: Ring et al. (1972) directly identified glutamic acid in discharge products. The check suggests NH3 as a possible coproduct and CO2 or CO as possible reactant