rk. Irradiation, ZnS properties, pH and substrate concentrations are unspecified. Transfer from the H2/Ni module requires demonstrated compatibility; sulfate remains a formal rather than measured exclusive oxidation endpoint.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify absorbed wavelengths, photon flux, ZnS loading, sulfite concentration and the approximately pH 9 operating regime.\",\n        \"Demonstrate activity with the delivered upstream mixture, including any necessary metal removal or pressure adjustment.\",\n        \"Measure sulfite oxidation products, ZnS stability and net free-glutamate accumulation under illumination.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.86\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.8,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.64,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.86,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support oxime formation followed by Fe0 reduction to glutamate. The four-electron reduction is correctly balanceable with two Fe atoms and four protons. Their integrated carbon-growth demonstration used Fe-promoted chemistry, however, and does not itself establish compatibility with this network's H2/Ni upstream module. Hydroxylamine supply is appropriately conditional. The mol_013 encoding inconsistency and unspecified reaction pH, temperature and iron passivation remain concerns.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct mol_013 and retain the explicit two-Fe stoichiometry for oxime reduction.\",\n        \"Specify oxime formation and reduction conditions, distinguishing proton b