ence time. The cited amination benchmark used 30 mM ketoacid, 5 bar H2, pH 8, 22\u00b0C and 72 h.\",\n        \"Provide the carbon-growth yield and glutamate yield for the proposed combined mixture, including competition from ketoacid hydrogenation and amination of upstream substrates.\",\n        \"Attach reaction-specific citations and clarify which integration details were experimentally demonstrated rather than inferred.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.92\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024, Chem) directly support glutamate formation using H2 and ground Aletai meteorite. Both listed reactions conserve atoms and charge. The network correctly describes this as a catalyst variant rather than an independent reaction family. However, meteoritic-metal amination does not by itself establish that the same material performs rxn_001 with the required productivity. The catalyst assignment and material handoff between the two modules remain unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify the catalyst used for rxn_001 separately from the meteoritic material used for rxn_003.\",\n        \"Specify meteoritic mineral composition, particle size, loading, surface preparation and H2 conditions consistent with the supporting experiment.\",\n        \"Document transfer of the ketoacid-containing solution and measure amino-acid versus hydroxyacid production in that actual feed mixture.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.89,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.91,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n      \"feedback\": \"The formate-supported reductive-amination equation is balanced and chemically credible. Kaur et al. (2024, Chem) support replacement of H2 by formate in Pd-catalyzed experiments. This establishes a valid conditional conversion of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate, but not the performance of the entire upstream mixture after delivery to a Pd-containing reservoir. No penalty is warranted merely for choosing Pd or formate; the uncertainty concerns the unspecified catalyst formulation, conditions and compatibility with the transferred solution.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the experimentally supported Pd formulation, loading, formate/ammonium concentrations, pH and incubation conditions.\",\n        \"Document whether upstream solids or dissolved components require removal before transfer to the Pd reservoir.\",\n        \"Report glutamate-specific yield rather than applying substrate-panel yield or selectivity ranges directly to glutamate.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.77\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.79,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.64,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.87,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019, Science Advances) strongly support glutamate production on partially electroreduced FeS. The supplied check confirms that rxn_005 balances with one Fe and two proton equivalents. Nevertheless, Fe(0) oxidation to dissolved Fe2+ is only formal electron accounting, not a resolved description of the prepared FeS surface or its oxidation products. The mol_011 formula/InChI inconsistency also requires correction. Declaring the prepared surface an external supply appropriately limits the claim, but does not specify its reduction capacity or persistence in the receiving reservoir.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct mol_011 so its formula and structure consistently represent a single Fe2+ ion, for example [Fe+2].\",\n        \"Retain the balanced formal equation but distinguish it explicitly from the mineral mechanism; identify the active reduced phase, available electron inventory and relevant oxidation products.\",\n        \"Specify surface preparation, loading and reaction conditions, including the relationship to the reported approximately \u22120.7 V versus SHE pretreatment and alkaline amination conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Wang et al. (2012, Chemical Communications) directly support illuminated-ZnS reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate, enhanced by sulfite. The listed sulfite-to-sulfate equation provides a valid formal two-electron balance. A light-exposed surface assignment is appropriate, but illumination spectrum, photon flux, ZnS properties, ammonia concentration and sulfite loading are absent. The reported approximately 3% glutamate yield with sulfite establishes target formation, not high throughput or reliable accumulation under arbitrary surface conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify an illuminated ZnS-containing receiving environment, including wavelength range, optical penetration, pH near the experimental value and reactant concentrations.\",\n        \"Document delivery of 2-oxoglutarate without dilution or competing photochemistry overwhelming its conversion.\",\n        \"Treat sulfate formation as formal donor accounting unless sulfur-product measurements support it, and quantify glutamate persistence during illumination.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.67,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by iron-driven reduction is chemically well supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, Nature). The oxime formula is consistent, and rxn_008 correctly requires a four-electron reduction: two Fe atoms and four proton equivalents in the supplied accounting. The hydroxylamine inventory is explicitly conditional, appropriately acknowledging the literature's supply concern. Remaining issues are the mol_011 structural inconsistency and unspecified compatibility between the H2-driven precursor module and the hydroxylamine/Fe reduction stage.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct mol_011 and encode the validated coefficients explicitly for rxn_008.\",\n        \"Specify hydroxylamine addition, Fe loading, pH, temperature and reaction time, distinguishing the selected H2-based carbon-growth module from the Fe-promoted network studied in 2019.\",\n        \"Measure oxime capture and glutamate release in the transferred mixture, including competing reduction or decomposition of hydroxylamine.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Strecker-to-glycine branch and subsequent transamination form a coherent, conservation-valid route. Stubbs et al. (2020, Nature Chemistry) support Al(III)-assisted glycine-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination, including substantial glutamate formation under concentrated, hot acidic conditions. Glyoxylate regeneration is stoichiometrically legitimate, and the network correctly requires an initial glyoxylate inventory. The main uncertainty is execution: aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis is not established simply by assigning it to a surface environment, and the required Al(III), concentration and conditioning details are missing.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify conditions and residence time sufficient to hydrolyze aminoacetonitrile to glycine; rxn_010 requires two water molecules per aminonitrile.\",\n        \"Define soluble Al(III) availability and conditions corresponding to the reported acidic, approximately 80\u00b0C transamination experiments.\",\n        \"Describe transfer and concentration of glycine and 2-oxoglutarate, including management of residual cyanide and maintenance of the required pH.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.86\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.86,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022, Nature Chemistry) directly support the hydantoin intermediate and subsequent free glutamate formation. All four equations pass the supplied balance check. CO2 appearing on both sides of rxn_013 is not a conservation error, although its mechanistic and bicarbonate-buffer roles should be explained. The principal limitations are quantitative DAP deamidation and slow hydantoin hydrolysis: the cited experiment produced 96% hydantoin but only 19% glutamate after a further 14 days at 80\u00b0C. These are execution and throughput concerns, not evidence that glutamate cannot form.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the concentration mechanism and retention conditions needed for the reported approximately 0.35 M ketoacid, concentrated cyanide/DAP and bicarbonate mixture.\",\n        \"Measure DAP deamidation and ammonia availability during incubation rather than assuming its balanced complete hydrolysis occurs quantitatively on the required timescale.\",\n        \"Represent the room-temperature hydantoin-forming incubation and prolonged hot hydrolysis explicitly, tracking residual hydantoin separately from free glutamate.\",\n        \"Clarify carbon accounting and CO2 exchange in rxn_013 without deleting bicarbonate's experimentally relevant role.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.7\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.79,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.74,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a chemically credible 2-oxoglutarate-independent route, consistent with Van Trump and Miller (1972, Science) and the acrolein/cyanide experiments of Fern\u00e1ndez-Garc\u00eda, Grefenstette and Powner (2017, Chemical Communications). The cyanohydrin and aminodinitrile formulas are consistent with the proposed skeleton, and all three reactions balance. However, efficient cyanohydrin formation does not establish efficient completion of the pathway. Cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion requires the appropriate aldehyde/imine equilibria, while exhaustive hydrolysis must be distinguished from accumulation of amides or glutamine-related products.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify concentrations and pH against the reported acrolein/cyanide benchmark of approximately 140 mM acrolein, 700 mM cyanide and pH 9.2.\",\n        \"Explain rxn_016 through reversible cyanohydrin dissociation and Strecker chemistry rather than implying an automatically efficient direct hydroxyl-to-amino substitution.\",\n        \"Provide a validated hydrolysis schedule and analytical evidence for free glutamate. The balanced rxn_017 consumes four waters and releases two ammonia equivalents.\",\n        \"Track partially hydrolyzed intermediates and competing products during the conditioning needed for complete nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.05\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.05,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.25,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.4,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.3,\n      \"feedback\": \"Ring et al. (1972, PNAS) provide direct evidence that methane/nitrogen/water/trace-ammonia discharge experiments can produce glutamic acid. Nevertheless, the supplied deterministic check classifies rxn_018 as UNBALANCED, so this submitted reaction is a conservation violation and cannot validate P10 as encoded. Calling the equation formal elemental accounting does not waive conservation. The checker hints at NH3 as an additional product and CO2 or CO as additional reactants; its H2-product hint is not